{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n22 \n \n \nthose images are typically selected based on non-imaging data to provide extreme examples.  The \nreview helps to verify parameters or plan for adjustments in subsequent tests, e.g., to select the \nvoltage range after determining where the ion current saturates for the given flame conditions.  \nAfter a test is conducted, a fan circulates the chamber atmosphere after which the chamber is \nvented (to space) back to atmospheric pressure, if needed.  Midway through a day\u2019s E-FIELD \nFlames tests, the atmosphere is circulated for 15 minutes through a filter cartridge filled with \nmolecular sieves to capture any soot and adsorb at least a portion of the combustion products, \ne.g., carbon dioxide, water vapor, and incomplete combustion products.  The tests are only \nconducted when both Ku and S band communication is available through NASA\u2019s Tracking and \nData Relay Satellite (TDRS) System.  Depending on the various orbits, there are periodic \ncommunication outages with a loss of signal (LOS) that can be a short handover (from one satellite \nto another) or can last as long as 30 minutes. \n \nAn average of 16 tests were conducted per Flame Design test day, where ignition was typically \nunsuccessful (e.g., un-sustained) in a couple of tests. \n \nIn contrast to E-FIELD Flames, recirculation during Flame Design inverse-flame test operations was \nonly conducted prior to venting at the end of a test day.  During normal-flame testing, it was \nusually conducted once in the midst of testing, as done with E-FIELD Flames.  Exceptions were test \ndays 19115, 19123, and 19137 (the first three days with flames); 19161 (when no flames were \nignited); 19206 (the last test day in the first round of testing), and 21301 (the first day of the second \nround of testing).  Another exception was on 19150 when it was carried out during the midst of \ntesting and then again when the combustion chamber was vented and refilled for additional tests \non the same day. \n \nA variety of constraints limit the number of tests that can be performed, both overall and on a \ndaily basis.  The next combustion experiments in the CIR will feature solid fuels and focus on flame \nspread and material flammability.  That follow-on plan limits the duration of ACME operations, \nwhere each ACME experiment is allotted four months for testing.  Although it requires additional", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 22}}